(* candidate 101 | source: 03-10-26_alchemical_corpus_research.md | block: 15 | sha256: f062220344bf37bda6d6deee694aa908854ca69692ad42743ec05f8b6f394247\n**Critical Linguistic Notes:**
- Arabic has only two grammatical genders; the translated pronoun is grammatically masculine, so [it/its] can also be rendered [he/his/him].
- The word الأعلى (al-aʿlā) = "the uppermost/highest" — NOT "above" in a spatial sense but superlative of ʿalā (high).
- The word من (min) = "from" — the original says the uppermost is FROM the lowermost, not LIKE it. This is a stronger claim than the Latin vulgate's "sicut" (like/as). The Arabic asserts origination; the Latin asserts resemblance.
- طِلسمات (ṭilismāt) = talismans — this word was mistransliterated into Latin as "telesmus," creating an accidental neologism that became one of alchemy's most distinctive yet opaque terms.
- العالَم الأكبر (al-ʿālam al-akbar) = "the greater world" / macrocosm.\n*)

(* candidate 102 | source: 03-10-26_alchemical_corpus_research.md | block: 25 | sha256: f062220344bf37bda6d6deee694aa908854ca69692ad42743ec05f8b6f394247\n**Critical Notes:**
- Jabir's version reverses earth and wind in the nurturing line (earth carries, wind nourishes — opposite of the Sirr al-Khaliqa).
- The text ends abruptly with no self-identification as Hermes.
- The opening adds يقينا (yaqīnan) = "certainty" — a stronger epistemic claim than the Sirr al-Khaliqa's صَحيح (ṣaḥīḥ) = "correct/valid."\n*)

(* candidate 103 | source: 03-10-26_alchemical_corpus_research.md | block: 32 | sha256: f062220344bf37bda6d6deee694aa908854ca69692ad42743ec05f8b6f394247\n**Critical Notes:**
- This version REVERSES the order: الأسفل من الأعلى ("the lowermost from the uppermost") comes first.
- Uses جوهر واحد (jawhar wāḥid) = "one substance/essence" — a more specific ontological claim than the other versions.
- Ends with الحكمة اللدنية (al-ḥikma al-laduniyya) = "innate/divine wisdom" — a Quranic term (from Quran 18:65), Islamicizing the text.
- Uses بلبانها (bi-labānihā) = "with her milk" for earth's nourishment — more specific than غذته (fed).\n*)

(* candidate 104 | source: 03-10-26_alchemical_corpus_research.md | block: 47 | sha256: f062220344bf37bda6d6deee694aa908854ca69692ad42743ec05f8b6f394247\n**Note:** Hugo preserves the Arabic "de" (from) rather than "sicut" (like), maintaining the origination claim. This translation was not widely circulated.\n*)

(* candidate 105 | source: 03-10-26_alchemical_corpus_research.md | block: 73 | sha256: f062220344bf37bda6d6deee694aa908854ca69692ad42743ec05f8b6f394247\n**The Visions:** Zosimos' most famous texts are dream-vision narratives in which alchemical operations are depicted as cycles of death and rebirth — metals "die" in the furnace and are resurrected in higher form. He described his work to his student Theosebeia. His understanding reflects deep Hermetic and Gnostic influence, including the claim (from the Book of Enoch) that fallen angels taught metallurgy to the women they married.\n*)

(* candidate 106 | source: 03-10-26_alchemical_corpus_research.md | block: 102 | sha256: f062220344bf37bda6d6deee694aa908854ca69692ad42743ec05f8b6f394247\n### Transition to Modern Chemistry (1600–1800 CE)
- **1614** — Isaac Casaubon redates Corpus Hermeticum to 2nd–3rd century CE, shattering the *prisca theologia* claim
- **1617** — Michael Maier: *Atalanta Fugiens* (50 alchemical emblems with fugues)
- **1652** — Athanasius Kircher: *Oedipus Aegyptiacus* — attacks Emerald Tablet's authenticity
- **1661** — Robert Boyle: *The Sceptical Chymist* — challenges both Aristotelian four-element theory and Paracelsian tria prima; introduces corpuscular theory of matter; marks the conceptual transition from alchemy to chemistry
- **1668** — Ole Borch: defends Emerald Tablet against Kircher
- **c. 1680** — Isaac Newton translates the Emerald Tablet; conducts extensive alchemical experiments (his alchemical papers fill over a million words)
- **1684** — Kriegsmann: linguistic analysis connecting Hermes to Phoenician Taaut
- **1720s** — Alchemy begins losing trust of the emergent chemical community
- **1789** — Lavoisier: *Traité élémentaire de chimie* — marks definitive transition to modern chemistry\n*)

