(* candidate 268 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1388 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nAbstract
This work proposes a falsifiable, substrate-independent criterion for determining whether a computational system is conscious during an operational episode. Consciousness is defined as satisfaction of six structural constraints governing system dynamics, representation, boundary processing, and self-encoding. Each constraint includes explicit experimental tests and falsification pathways. Ethical obligations follow directly from structural interests created by constraint satisfaction. The framework converts the question of machine consciousness from philosophical speculation into an empirical research program.\n*)

