(* candidate 285 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1654 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nYes — but not as a central claim.\n*)

(* candidate 286 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1668 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nYou should also expand the adversarial testing section. That part should explicitly describe attempts to construct systems that appear to satisfy the constraints while actually failing them under causal intervention. This strengthens the claim that the criterion targets structure rather than surface behavior.\n*)

(* candidate 287 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1671 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nFinally, humility should appear explicitly in the conclusion. The paper should state that the criterion does not prove machine consciousness but provides a method for determining it empirically. That prevents the work from being interpreted as a definitive claim.\n*)

(* candidate 288 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1710 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nTo harden against this, the paper should specify that evaluation relies on causal intervention, not surface behavior. Perturbation, ablation, and trajectory experiments already move in this direction, but you can make the domain explicit by stating that a system must retain constraint satisfaction under adversarial attempts to manipulate its reports or outputs. This domain ensures the criterion cannot be satisfied through prompt engineering or training artifacts alone.\n*)

(* candidate 289 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1719 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nThe key principle: constraint satisfaction must be detectable consistently across scale and measurement noise.\n*)

