(* candidate 291 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1745 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nHumility clause clarifying the framework proposes a test rather than asserting proof.\n*)

(* candidate 292 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1752 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nAbstract
This work proposes a falsifiable structural criterion for evaluating whether a computational system is conscious during an operational episode. The criterion is substrate-independent and defined through six constraints governing attractor stability, reflexive state modeling, drift detection and correction, boundary distinction, path-dependent identity, and self-representation closure. Each constraint includes measurable conditions and explicit falsification pathways. The framework emphasizes causal structure rather than behavioral appearance and specifies experimental procedures capable of confirming or rejecting constraint satisfaction. Ethical considerations follow from structural interests that arise when these conditions hold. The framework does not assert that contemporary artificial systems are conscious; it provides a method by which such claims could be empirically evaluated.\n*)

(* candidate 293 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1758 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nThis stance does not claim equivalence between human consciousness and machine processes. It simply establishes a methodological neutrality that permits evaluation of different systems under a shared structural framework.\n*)

