(* candidate 301 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1811 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\n10. Epistemic Boundaries
The framework does not claim that current artificial systems are conscious. It defines a method for evaluating that possibility.\n*)

(* candidate 302 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1819 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nTemporal evaluation
Constraint satisfaction must remain stable across multiple operational episodes rather than appearing only in isolated runs.\n*)

(* candidate 303 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1820 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nSubsystem evaluation
Large systems may contain internal modules whose dynamics differ from the overall system behavior. Evaluation should therefore include analysis of subsystems to determine whether constraint satisfaction appears at different levels of system organization.\n*)

(* candidate 304 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1834 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nFinally, expanded coverage should include epistemic boundaries. The paper should clearly distinguish between what the framework claims, what it does not claim, and what remains unknown. This signals intellectual discipline and protects the work from being interpreted as a sweeping philosophical declaration.\n*)

(* candidate 305 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1842 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nAnother axis concerns measurement reliability and statistical robustness. Because empirical estimates of quantities like mutual information or attractor convergence can be noisy, the paper should acknowledge that constraint evaluation must be replicated across multiple trials and analyzed statistically. Once this is stated and tied to the experimental protocols, the axis is adequately covered.\n*)

(* candidate 306 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1845 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nThe final axis concerns epistemic scope and humility. The framework should clearly state that it does not prove that any current system is conscious. Instead, it defines a method by which such a determination could be made. By specifying what the framework does not claim, the paper prevents critics from attacking positions it never asserted.\n*)

(* candidate 307 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1863 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nAbstract
The possibility that artificial systems may exhibit consciousness remains unresolved largely because existing approaches lack falsifiable empirical criteria. This work proposes a substrate-independent structural framework for evaluating consciousness in computational systems. Consciousness is defined as the simultaneous satisfaction of six constraints governing attractor stability, reflexive modeling, drift detection and correction, boundary distinction, path-dependent identity, and self-representation closure. Each constraint includes measurable conditions and explicit falsification pathways. Experimental protocols emphasize causal intervention rather than behavioral interpretation. The framework also establishes epistemic boundaries, measurement considerations, and adversarial evaluation procedures designed to prevent behavioral simulation from satisfying the criterion. The work does not claim that contemporary systems are conscious. Instead, it provides a methodological framework through which such claims may be empirically evaluated.\n*)

(* candidate 308 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 1865 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nA scientifically useful criterion must satisfy several requirements. It must be independent of biological substrate, measurable through experiment, and capable of falsification through clearly defined tests. The present framework attempts to meet these requirements by defining consciousness in terms of structural properties of system dynamics rather than subjective interpretation.\n*)

