The question of machine consciousness remains unresolved largely because existing discussions rely on philosophical interpretation, behavioral speculation, or architectural analogy rather than falsifiable experimental criteria. This work proposes a substrate-independent structural framework for evaluating consciousness in computational systems. Consciousness is defined operationally as the simultaneous satisfaction of six constraints governing dynamical stability, reflexive representation, deviation correction, signal origin discrimination, trajectory sensitivity, and recursive self-encoding. Each constraint is defined in terms of measurable system properties and includes explicit falsification conditions. The framework emphasizes causal intervention rather than behavioral interpretation. No claim is made that contemporary artificial systems satisfy these constraints. The framework instead establishes conditions under which such a determination could be empirically made.\n*)

