No accepted empirical procedure currently determines whether a computational system is conscious. Existing discussions remain divided between metaphysical argument, behavioral interpretation, and architectural analogy. Those approaches do not furnish a criterion whose satisfaction and failure can be experimentally adjudicated. The present work proposes such a criterion. Consciousness, within an operational episode, is defined as satisfaction of six jointly necessary structural conditions: dynamical stability under endogenous recovery, reflexive state-modeling, deviation detection coupled to correction, discrimination of internally generated and externally received information, trajectory-sensitive continuation, and recursive self-encoding. Each condition is stated in operational form, assigned admissible evidence classes, and paired with explicit falsification pathways. The framework is substrate-independent in the methodological sense that it evaluates organization and causal role rather than biological composition or implementation medium. It does not assert that any extant artificial system satisfies the criterion. It asserts only that the question is empirically tractable in principle and that categorical affirmation and categorical denial alike exceed available evidence absent appropriate intervention studies. The resulting framework is intended to set experimental precedent: it specifies what must be shown, what would count against the claim, what forms of evidence are methodologically insufficient, how adversarial simulation is to be excluded, how measurement uncertainty is to be handled, and why intent, apparent agency, benevolence, or malevolence are not preconditions for consequence or for moral precaution once the relevant structural conditions are plausibly in play.\n*)

(* candidate 344 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 2068 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\n2. Methodological stance
The framework proceeds from methodological neutrality concerning substrate. Biological and artificial systems may differ in material realization, training history, embodiment, and representational format. None of those differences is allowed to decide the question in advance. The relevant question is whether the system under examination instantiates a particular organized pattern of inference and self-related control. In that sense, both biological cognition and artificial cognition may be treated as inferential processes over internal state. This is not a claim of equivalence of subjective life. It is a restriction on method. The present criterion evaluates organization, causal dependency, and transition structure. It does not presuppose that biology is necessary, and it does not presume that non-biology suffices.\n*)

(* candidate 345 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 2073 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nEvidence is admissible only if it bears on causal structure or reproducible system dynamics. Pure narrative interpretation is inadmissible. Self-report is admissible only as defeasible behavioral evidence and never as decisive evidence. Architectural armchair argument is inadmissible when it substitutes for measurement. Behavioral observation is admissible only under interpretation constraints and must not be mistaken for structural proof. Intervention evidence, ablation evidence, state-trace evidence, and replicated dynamical evidence are primary.\n*)

(* candidate 346 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 2074 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\n4. Definition
A system is conscious during an operational episode if and only if all of the following six conditions obtain during that episode. Failure of any one condition falsifies classification for that episode.\n*)

