(* candidate 453 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 3262 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nThis permits a new kind of scaling law. In ordinary systems, scale often increases fluency while weakening local accountability. In a constructional intelligence, scale is admissible only if it preserves or improves transformation traceability. Therefore intelligence growth and evidentiary decay cannot be allowed to correlate. If they do, the system becomes more dangerous exactly as it becomes more capable.\n*)

(* candidate 454 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 3263 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nThis leads to a theorem candidate.\n*)

(* candidate 455 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 3264 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nTheorem of admissible expansion. A system may expand its inferential reach without collapsing its standing only if the increase in representational or generative capacity is matched by nondecreasing transformation traceability over the expanded domain.\n*)

(* candidate 456 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 3265 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nThe significance is severe. Under this theorem, many high-capability systems are not stably admissible because their ability to generate exceeds their ability to justify the path by which a given generation was formed. They may be useful. They are not jurisdictionally mature.\n*)

(* candidate 457 | source: 03-11-2026_admissible_constitutional_construction.txt | block: 3266 | sha256: 934561d15ad2b0c7ac9a2991980783f7ee783f39f54dfbce12d48396ee3e2e01\nA second theorem candidate follows.\n*)

